outputs.L1:
A flexible pipe connector (200) for joining a pressurised mains pipe to a service pipe, the connector comprising:

• a corrugated longitudinal body portion (202) having a first end (208), a second end (210), and an internal bore extending therebetween, said longitudinal body portion further comprising a plurality of corrugations spaced along its length, the corrugations defining a maximum internal diameter of the bore at a peak (204) of a corrugation and a minimum internal diameter of the bore at a trough (206) of a corrugation;

• a first spigot socket (212) at said first end of the longitudinal body portion;

• a second spigot socket (214) at said second end of the longitudinal body portion;

• wherein, for a defined volumetric flow rate, the head loss of a fluid flowing through said body portion is no greater than the head loss that would occur if the fluid were flowing through a smooth-bored pipe of the same length and having the same internal diameter as the first and second spigot sockets; and

• wherein the minimum internal diameter of said corrugated internal bore is greater than the maximum internal diameter of said first and second spigots.

outputs.L2:
A flexible pipe coupling (200) configured to connect a pressurised main pipe with a service pipe, the coupling including:

- an elongate corrugated section (202) with a first end (208), an opposite second end (210), and an internal passage extending between them, the elongate section having multiple corrugations arranged along its length, each corrugation forming a largest bore diameter at a corrugation crest (204) and a smallest bore diameter at a corrugation valley (206);
- a first spigot-receiving socket (212) positioned at the first end of the elongate section; and
- a second spigot-receiving socket (214) positioned at the second end of the elongate section;

wherein, at a specified volume flow rate, fluid passing through the elongate section experiences a head loss that is not greater than the head loss that would occur in a smooth-bore pipe of equal length and with an internal diameter matching that of the first and second spigot sockets; and

wherein the smallest internal diameter of the corrugated bore is larger than the largest internal diameter of the first and second spigots.

counterfactuals.output:
1. A flexible corrugated pipe connector for joining a pressurised mains pipe to a service pipe, the connector comprising:

a tubular body defining a through-bore extending along a longitudinal axis between a first end and a second end;

a first spigot socket at the first end configured to receive and sealingly engage a spigot or pipe end associated with the pressurised mains pipe;

a second spigot socket at the second end configured to receive and sealingly engage a spigot or pipe end associated with the service pipe; and

an intermediate flexible corrugated portion between the first and second spigot sockets, the intermediate flexible corrugated portion comprising a plurality of annular corrugations configured to permit angular and/or axial deflection of the connector,

wherein the through-bore extends continuously through the first spigot socket, the intermediate flexible corrugated portion and the second spigot socket, and wherein an internal surface of the intermediate flexible corrugated portion is configured such that, during flow of pressurised fluid from the mains pipe to the service pipe, the corrugated through-bore produces no greater head loss than an equivalent smooth-bore pipe connector having substantially the same internal diameter, length and flow conditions.

1. A low-head-loss flexible pipe connector for connection between a pressurised water main and a service pipe, the connector comprising:  

   a flexible corrugated body having an internal bore extending therethrough;  

   a first end spigot at a first end of the flexible corrugated body for connection to the pressurised water main; and  

   a second end spigot at a second end of the flexible corrugated body for connection to the service pipe;  

   wherein the first and second end spigots each define a respective internal passage having a maximum internal diameter, and wherein the corrugated internal bore of the flexible corrugated body has, along the corrugated portion thereof, a minimum internal diameter greater than the maximum internal diameter of each of the respective internal passages of the first and second end spigots, thereby reducing head loss through the connector while permitting flexure between the pressurised water main and the service pipe.

A flexible connector comprising:

a corrugated body portion defining an internal bore for conveying fluid, the corrugated body portion having a longitudinal axis and a plurality of circumferential corrugations providing flexibility;

first and second spigot sockets disposed at respective opposite ends of the corrugated body portion, each spigot socket being configured to receive or connect to a corresponding pipe spigot and having a socket internal diameter;

wherein the corrugated body portion has an effective flow area and corrugation geometry selected relative to the socket internal diameter such that, for fluid flow through the flexible connector between the first and second spigot sockets, the head loss through the flexible connector is no greater than the head loss through a straight smooth-bored pipe having the same overall length as the flexible connector and an internal diameter equal to the socket internal diameter.
